1	/*
2	 * check-conventions.mjs — the design system's measurable rules, run by
3	 * `npm run lint` after `tsc --noEmit`.
4	 *
5	 * ## behavior
6	 * - `node scripts/check-conventions.mjs` runs every check in CHECKS over src/
7	 *   (.ts/.tsx/.css). Each violation prints one line,
8	 *   `FAIL <check name>: <file:line> <match>`, then each failed check prints its
9	 *   one-line why, and the script exits 1. With no violation it prints
10	 *   `conventions: N checks passed` and exits 0.
11	 * - `--fixtures [dir]` (dir defaults to scripts/check-conventions.fixtures/)
12	 *   runs the checks that have fixtures over that dir instead of src/, with the
13	 *   dir's allowlists.json in place of the allowlists below. It exits 0 only when
14	 *   every fixture file `<check-id>[.<case>].<ext>` is reported exactly once, by
15	 *   the check with that id, every other file (`*.ok.*` near-misses and support
16	 *   files) reports nothing, and every check reports at least one file. See the
17	 *   fixture dir's README.txt.
18	 * - Repo-level checks (`fixtures: false`, e.g. the generated-theme check) run
19	 *   only in the normal mode, because they read fixed paths, not the scan root.
20	 * - Paths are root-relative for scoping (`components/Text/`, `styles/tokens.css`)
21	 *   and repo-relative in output, so the same scoping holds in fixture mode.
22	 *
23	 * ## Adding a check
24	 * Append ONE object to CHECKS, nothing else:
25	 *   { id, name, why, run(ctx) => Violation[] }          // code check: add a fixture
26	 *   { id, name, why, fixtures: false, run(ctx) => [...] } // repo-level check
27	 * - `id` is kebab-case; it names the check's fixture files.
28	 * - `name` and `why` are quoted verbatim by the maintainer skills.
29	 * - A Violation is { file, line, match }. `file` is repo-relative (use `f.path`
30	 *   for a scanned file, or `repoRel(abs)` for anything else).
31	 * - `ctx.files` holds every scanned file as { path, rel, ts, css, story,
32	 *   generated, raw, code } where `code` is the source with comments blanked out
33	 *   (offsets and line numbers unchanged). Match `code`, never `raw`. Helpers:
34	 *   `scan(files, regex)` (every match is a violation) and `lineOf(code, index)`.
35	 * - A repo-level check that wraps a generator calls the generator's own
36	 *   write-nothing `--check` mode with `runCheckMode()`, as the generated-theme
37	 *   check does with sync-theme.mjs.
38	 *
39	 * ## constraints
40	 * - Check names and whys are a contract: the maintainer skills cite them word
41	 *   for word ("lint check: text-style classes outside Text components").
42	 *   Renaming one means updating every skill that cites it, in the same change.
43	 * - Code checks match comment-blanked source. A header contract must be able to
44	 *   name a forbidden class without failing lint, and line numbers must stay true.
45	 * - Lint stays fast: no browser, no build, no network, nothing written. A repo
46	 *   check calls a generator only through its in-memory `--check` mode.
47	 * - A regex fix that removes a false positive keeps its fixture catching the
48	 *   real violation; add the near-miss to that fixture so it cannot come back.
49	 * - Every allowlist entry carries a reason and `needs`, the APIs the reason
50	 *   names. An entry whose file is gone, lost its directive or timer, or no
51	 *   longer uses what `needs` names FAILS, so the list cannot rot. Fix the entry
52	 *   (or the code), never the matching, to make it pass.
53	 */
